7
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:35,160
Earlier in the series, when we introduced a variety of glassware in the chemistry laboratory,

8
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:41,080
we talked a little bit about the purposes intended for each, including volume measurement.

9
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:45,640
Beakers and flasks have volume markings on them like these.

10
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:50,760
We may be tempted to think that this is suitable for measuring 150 milliliters of fluid, for

11
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:56,200
example, but any such measurements will be rather inaccurate because these are approximate

12
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:57,360
values.

13
00:00:57,360 --> 00:01:02,480
The width of the vessel means that a small error contributes to a large difference in volume

14
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:03,800
that will be read.

15
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:09,060
Graduated cylinders are much better for measuring volume because the cylinders are narrower,

16
00:01:09,060 --> 00:01:12,400
and they have more volume markings to minimize errors.

17
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:17,880
However, they also have their limits when it comes to small volumes.

18
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:22,520
Specific flasks are another good option for measuring volume because this line here will

19
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:27,920
indicate that, in this instance, you have precisely 250 milliliters, and it's hard

20
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:29,840
to generate a lot of error.

21
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:33,480
But this limits us to just one specific volume.

22
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:37,920
Therefore, we need a more accurate way to measure volumes.

23
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:39,980
Pipettes are perfect for this.

24
00:01:39,980 --> 00:01:45,440
We use them to accurately measure a wide range of volumes, from just a few microliters

25
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:48,280
up to tens of milliliters.

26
00:01:48,280 --> 00:01:53,000
Pipettes come in different shapes and sizes, but they can be defined as lab instruments

27
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,480
which can measure small volumes of liquid in an accurate and efficient manner.

28
00:01:57,480 --> 00:02:01,800
They are usually comprised of a tube and an aspirator at the top.

29
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:07,040
You can then stick it in the liquid, aspirate it up to the volume you want, and then transfer

30
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:11,120
that volume to another container by releasing the aspirator.

31
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:14,260
But these can sometimes be very different from one another.

32
00:02:14,260 --> 00:02:19,100
For example, in chemistry labs we like to use glass pipettes like these.

33
00:02:19,100 --> 00:02:23,780
They can transfer volumes from 1 milliliter up to tens of milliliters.

34
00:02:23,780 --> 00:02:27,660
To use these we push the top end into a bulb like this.

35
00:02:27,660 --> 00:02:31,980
We can then press the bulb to let the air out, and keep it pressed.

36
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:37,100
When you then place this in a liquid, releasing the bulb slowly will bring the liquid into

37
00:02:37,100 --> 00:02:41,900
the pipette, and you can stop when the liquid reaches your desired volume.

38
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:46,060
You can then transfer this to the desired container and push the bulb to let your measured

39
00:02:46,060 --> 00:02:49,150
liquid out.

40
00:02:49,150 --> 00:02:54,190
To make this more accurate, we use pumps like these so that you can push slowly.

41
00:02:54,190 --> 00:02:59,230
An interesting innovation is these automatic pipettes, which will aspirate and release

42
00:02:59,230 --> 00:03:02,130
your liquid with a push of a button.

43
00:03:02,130 --> 00:03:06,570
These are great for when you're doing a lot of repetitive measurements in one experiment

44
00:03:06,570 --> 00:03:08,590
since it can get tiring.

45
00:03:08,590 --> 00:03:12,850
When it comes to smaller volumes, we use different types of pipettes.

46
00:03:12,850 --> 00:03:18,050
The most common pipettes in a chem lab are these disposable pasture pipettes, which are

47
00:03:18,050 --> 00:03:20,730
great for transferring small volumes.

48
00:03:20,730 --> 00:03:24,810
They can have markings on them so that you know how much you're transferring.

49
00:03:24,810 --> 00:03:29,990
We also have glass versions of these since some liquids don't work with plastic.

50
00:03:29,990 --> 00:03:34,530
We call these transfer pipettes, and they are not disposable, so be sure to clean them

51
00:03:34,530 --> 00:03:38,370
and store them appropriately when finished working with them.

52
00:03:38,390 --> 00:03:42,890
As you can see, some of these don't have markings, so we use them to transfer fluid

53
00:03:42,890 --> 00:03:48,750
into something that has markings, or to add solutions drop-wise, meaning slowly, one drop

54
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:49,850
at a time.

55
00:03:49,850 --> 00:03:55,810
A rule of thumb in the lab is that 20 drops from this will give you about 1 milliliter.

56
00:03:55,810 --> 00:03:58,690
But as you can imagine, this is not precisely accurate.

57
00:03:58,690 --> 00:04:04,690
That's why for smaller scale experiments, especially in analytical chemistry or biochemistry,

58
00:04:04,690 --> 00:04:07,450
we use these micropipettes.

59
00:04:07,450 --> 00:04:12,930
These can be very accurate all the way down to 1 microliter, and the measurements are repeatable,

60
00:04:12,930 --> 00:04:16,650
but they have to be calibrated regularly to stay accurate.

61
00:04:16,650 --> 00:04:21,650
These pipettes use the same principle of aspirating the volume and dispensing it.

62
00:04:21,650 --> 00:04:27,530
However with these ones, you set up the volume you want beforehand and attach a special disposable

63
00:04:27,530 --> 00:04:29,090
pipette tip.

64
00:04:29,090 --> 00:04:34,730
When ready to take up volume, you push the piston down and put your tip in the solution.

65
00:04:34,730 --> 00:04:39,050
When you release the piston, the pipette will only take in the set volume, so there

66
00:04:39,050 --> 00:04:42,490
is no need to try to stop at a certain volume.

67
00:04:42,490 --> 00:04:47,530
To illustrate how to get volume measurements, let's make a standard solution together,

68
00:04:47,530 --> 00:04:50,810
which we will use in an experiment later in the series.

69
00:04:50,810 --> 00:04:56,810
A standard solution is a solution containing a precisely known concentration of a chemical.

70
00:04:56,810 --> 00:05:01,150
These are incredibly important in chemistry, because these known concentrations can be

71
00:05:01,150 --> 00:05:05,230
used to measure concentrations of unknown substances.

72
00:05:05,230 --> 00:05:11,150
Today we will make a standard solution for potassium hydrogen phthalate, or KHP.

73
00:05:11,150 --> 00:05:15,750
This is a common acid used in chemistry labs for acid-based titrations.

74
00:05:15,750 --> 00:05:20,750
Let's make a 0.1 molar aqueous solution of KHP.

75
00:05:20,750 --> 00:05:26,990
To do that, let's weigh 5 grams of the powder and add it to a 500 milliliter beaker.

76
00:05:26,990 --> 00:05:32,150
Add a stir bar and about 200 milliliters of water to dissolve the powder.

77
00:05:32,150 --> 00:05:38,710
Now looking at the molar mass, we can do some calculations to see that exactly 250 milliliters

78
00:05:38,710 --> 00:05:46,910
of water needs to be added to 5 grams of KHP to make the solution 0.1 molar.

79
00:05:46,910 --> 00:05:52,310
So after we dissolve this in the beaker, we will want to transfer it to a 250 milliliter

80
00:05:52,310 --> 00:05:54,590
volumetric flask.

81
00:05:54,590 --> 00:06:00,390
We can transfer the solution to the volumetric flask using a glass funnel, pouring slowly

82
00:06:00,390 --> 00:06:01,390
and carefully.

83
00:06:01,390 --> 00:06:06,150
Let's then use a water bottle to clean the beaker so that all of our KHP has been

84
00:06:06,150 --> 00:06:08,150
transferred to the flask.

85
00:06:08,150 --> 00:06:12,610
To add the rest of the water, we can use a water bottle, but once we start approaching

86
00:06:12,610 --> 00:06:19,350
the final volume, we will want to use a transfer pipette to add the last few milliliters.

87
00:06:19,350 --> 00:06:25,730
Add water until the bottom of the meniscus touches the 250 milliliter line.

88
00:06:25,730 --> 00:06:30,610
You have now made a 0.1 molar standard of KHP.

89
00:06:30,610 --> 00:06:36,230
We will frequently be making standard solutions of around this volume in the chemistry lab.

90
00:06:36,230 --> 00:06:40,150
Smaller standards can be made by using micropipettes.

91
00:06:40,150 --> 00:06:44,270
With this technique understood, let's move forward and see how it can be applied in

92
00:06:44,270 --> 00:06:45,430
certain experiments.

